    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-feb-calculus-1999-p6.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Of course this problem can be done by brute force, differentiating and then using the half angle formula to find \\(\\sin\\) and \\(\\cos\\) of 15 , but there is a quicker way. \\(e^{i x}=\\cos x+i \\sin x\\), so \\(\\sin (3 x)\\) is the imaginary part of \\((\\cos x+i \\sin x)^{3}\\), which is \\(3 \\cos ^{2} x \\sin x-\\sin ^{3} x=3 \\sin x-4 \\sin ^{3} x\\), so the expression we are differentiating is just \\(\\frac{1}{3} \\sin (3 x)\\). Hence the derivative is \\(\\cos (3 x)\\), and \\(\\cos 45=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\).",
